> Please don't use ChatGPT to generate issue reports. LLMs often tell us things that are unrelated, outdated, or completely wrong, which just makes more work for us.
> 
> That said, I think this is a risky change. `PyErr_CheckSignals` and `Py_MakePendingCalls` are both documented as "do nothing if not in the main thread (and main interpreter)", so changing that will likely break something.
> 
> What's the use-case here? Mixing asynchronous notifications with multithreading is generally not a good idea and often leads to spurious deadlocks.

Thanks, everyone, for your considered and helpful responses.

The interrupt point is not inside arbitrary C code -- it's between syscall attempts, with the GIL held. The use case is in an embedded Python service with thread-per-connections, where threads run user-code and are subject to a timeout. PyThreadState_SetAsyncExc is the only proper mechanism available for a timeout manager to interrupt another thread.

For example, without the fix, 'time.sleep(100)' hangs for 100 seconds and the thread can't be interrupted early based on the master timeout for the thread, or by any other clean shutdown system.

We currently have a growing selection of monkey-patches to introduce or reduce a time parameter according to computed time-remaining covering: `time.sleep`, `select.select/poll/epoll`, `socket.recv/send/accept`, `os.read/write`, `threading.Event/Condition/Semaphore`, `subprocess.Popen.wait/communicate`, `queue.Queue.get/put`, but monkey-patching is a monkeys game, as is getting the monkey-patch to apply at the right place during loading/import.

For completeness, I also explored LD_PRELOAD, and post-load binary hot-patching for those that can't be monkey-patched (e.g.: `threading.Lock.acquire`, `threading.RLock.acquire`, and `os.waitpid`) which really is sick, but it all becomes unnecessary with a small, centralised CPython fix.

To address fully the valid point about the safety of "arbitrary C code", as the EINTR retry paths are between syscall attempts with the GIL held they are as safe as bytecode boundaries.

For example, pysleep in timemodule.c shows the pattern: `Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS` releases the GIL, `clock_nanosleep` blocks, `Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS` reacquires the GIL, then:

        if (err != EINTR) {
            errno = err;
            PyErr_SetFromErrno(PyExc_OSError);
            return -1;
        }

        /* sleep was interrupted by SIGINT */
        if (PyErr_CheckSignals()) {
            return -1;
        }

GIL held, no syscall in progress. The `async_exc` check would go right where `PyErr_CheckSignals()` already runs.

If a broad change is too risky, a targeted async_exc check in pysleep's EINTR handler would help in the common cases, and suitable points no doubt exist for `select.select/poll/epoll`, `socket.recv/send/accept`, `os.read/write`, `threading.Event/Condition/Semaphore`, `subprocess.Popen.wait/communicate`, `queue.Queue.get/put` and others I haven't yet found - but again, that is a monkeys game which could be addressed neatly in one place.

**Correction to the original report:** I listed `time.sleep`'s EINTR retry as calling `Py_MakePendingCalls()` but it actually calls `PyErr_CheckSignals()` -- the same as select and socket. I've updated the table in the original report. This means adding the check to `Py_MakePendingCalls()` alone would not cover `time.sleep`. The better place is `PyErr_CheckSignals`, which already runs GC on non-main threads (as @colesbury noted) before the main-thread signal gate. An async_exc check in the same region follows the established pattern -- work that runs on all threads, before the main-thread-only signal dispatch.

Your caution @ZeroIntensity  is proper and protects users from many of the infinite potentialities of C-code shenanigans - but this is not one. I think this is a well-defined use case, and adding the check would make `PyThreadState_SetAsyncExc` work consistently across all checkpoint types -- bytecode boundaries and EINTR retry paths alike, establishing a proper supported mode of operation without the need for C-code shenanigans -- both rewarding and facilitating good behaviour.

> couldn't we just skip locking whichever lock in a CRITICAL_SECTION2 is currently locked in the (previous) top-most critical section?

That would work for the specific code in deque, but it's not safe in general because if the thread state is detached and then attached, only the (new) top-most critical section is locked. For example:

```c
Py_BEGIN_CRITICAL_SECTION(a);
Py_BEGIN_CRITICAL_SECTION2(a, b); // only locks b?

_PyThreadState_Detach(tstate);
_PyThreadState_Attach(tstate);

// it's important that both a and b are locked here.
// if the Py_BEGIN_CRITICAL_SECTION2 only pushed
// a critical section containing b, then only b will be locked

Py_END_CRITICAL_SECTION2();
Py_END_CRITICAL_SECTION();
```

I think we could probably get something to work safely along the lines of "only lock b", but I think it'll be complicated.

Quick & dirty patch fixing the issue for me on a regular Python build (with the GIL):

```diff
diff --git a/Objects/obmalloc.c b/Objects/obmalloc.c
index b59ebdfbda3..4951f6697b2 100644
--- a/Objects/obmalloc.c
+++ b/Objects/obmalloc.c
@@ -244,7 +244,12 @@ _PyMem_MiMalloc(void *ctx, size_t size)
     mi_heap_t *heap = &tstate->mimalloc.heaps[_Py_MIMALLOC_HEAP_MEM];
     return mi_heap_malloc(heap, size);
 #else
-    return mi_malloc(size);
+    if (size > 8) {
+        return mi_malloc(size);
+    }
+    else {
+        return mi_malloc_aligned(size, 8);
+    }
 #endif
 }

@@ -268,7 +273,12 @@ _PyMem_MiRealloc(void *ctx, void *ptr, size_t size)
     mi_heap_t *heap = &tstate->mimalloc.heaps[_Py_MIMALLOC_HEAP_MEM];
     return mi_heap_realloc(heap, ptr, size);
 #else
-    return mi_realloc(ptr, size);
+    if (size > 8) {
+        return mi_realloc(ptr, size);
+    }
+    else {
+        return mi_realloc_aligned(ptr, size, 8);
+    }
 #endif
 }

```

My hack uses mimalloc functions with an alignment of 8 bytes for allocations smaller than 8 bytes.

And I wrote a code to return all of the module name. However, I don't know whether the `sys.builtin_module_names` different, so it may be changed by `#ifdef ...`:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#ifdef _WIN32
    #include <windows.h>
#else
    #include <dirent.h>
#endif
#include <sys/stat.h>

// gcc list_modules.c -DPYTHON_STD_PATH=\"/usr/lib/python3.11\" -o list_modules
// gcc list_modules.c -DPY_EXT_PREFIX=\"cpython-311-x86_64-linux-gnu\" -o list_modules

#define PY_EXT_PY ".py"

#ifdef _WIN32
    #define PY_EXT_PYD ".pyd"
#else
    #define PY_EXT_SO ".so"
#endif

const char* builtin_modules[] = {
    "_abc", "_ast", "_bisect", "_blake2", "_codecs", "_codecs_cn",
    "_codecs_hk", "_codecs_iso2022", "_codecs_jp", "_codecs_kr",
    "_codecs_tw", "_collections", "_contextvars", "_csv", "_datetime",
    "_functools", "_heapq", "_imp", "_interpchannels", "_interpqueues",
    "_interpreters", "_io", "_json", "_locale", "_lsprof", "_md5", 
    "_multibytecodec", "_opcode",  "_operator", "_pickle", "_random",
    "_sha1", "_sha2", "_sha3", "_signal", "_sre", "_stat", "_statistics",
    "_string", "_struct", "_suggestions", "_symtable", "_sysconfig", "_thread",
    "_tokenize", "_tracemalloc", "_typing", "_warnings", "_weakref", "_winapi",
    "array", "atexit", "binascii", "builtins", "cmath", "errno", "faulthandler",
    "gc", "itertools", "marshal", "math", "mmap", "msvcrt", "nt", "sys", "time",
    "winreg", "xxsubtype", "zlib",
    NULL
};

int has_suffix(const char *name, const char *suffix) {
    size_t len1 = strlen(name);
    size_t len2 = strlen(suffix);
    return len1 > len2 && strcmp(name + len1 - len2, suffix) == 0;
}

#ifdef PY_EXT_PREFIX
int match_ext_prefix(const char* filename) {
    return strstr(filename, PY_EXT_PREFIX) != NULL;
}
#endif

void add_module_name(const char* filename) {
    printf("%s\n", filename);
}

void scan_dir(const char* path) {
#ifdef _WIN32
    WIN32_FIND_DATA findFileData;
    HANDLE hFind;
    char searchPath[MAX_PATH];

    snprintf(searchPath, sizeof(searchPath), "%s\\*", path);
    hFind = FindFirstFile(searchPath, &findFileData);

    if (hFind == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) return;

    do {
        if (strcmp(findFileData.cFileName, ".") == 0 || 
            strcmp(findFileData.cFileName, "..") == 0) continue;

        char fullpath[MAX_PATH];
        snprintf(fullpath, sizeof(fullpath), "%s\\%s", path, findFileData.cFileName);

        if (findFileData.dwFileAttributes & FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY) {
            char initfile[MAX_PATH];
            snprintf(initfile, sizeof(initfile), "%s\\__init__.py", fullpath);
            WIN32_FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DATA fileData;
            if (GetFileAttributesEx(initfile, GetFileExInfoStandard, &fileData)) {
                add_module_name(findFileData.cFileName);
            }
        } else {
            if (has_suffix(findFileData.cFileName, PY_EXT_PY)) {
                size_t len = strlen(findFileData.cFileName) - strlen(PY_EXT_PY);
                char modname[256];
                strncpy(modname, findFileData.cFileName, len);
                modname[len] = '\0';
                add_module_name(modname);
            }
#ifdef PY_EXT_PREFIX
            else if (has_suffix(findFileData.cFileName, PY_EXT_PYD) && match_ext_prefix(findFileData.cFileName)) {
                size_t len = strlen(findFileData.cFileName) - strlen(PY_EXT_PYD);
                char modname[256];
                strncpy(modname, findFileData.cFileName, len);
                modname[len] = '\0';
                add_module_name(modname);
            }
#endif
        }
    } while (FindNextFile(hFind, &findFileData));

    FindClose(hFind);
#else
    DIR *dir = opendir(path);
    if (!dir) return;
    struct dirent *entry;
    while ((entry = readdir(dir)) != NULL) {
        if (entry->d_name[0] == '.') continue;
        char fullpath[1024];
        snprintf(fullpath, sizeof(fullpath), "%s/%s", path, entry->d_name);

        struct stat st;
        if (stat(fullpath, &st) == -1) continue;

        if (S_ISREG(st.st_mode)) {
            // .py
            if (has_suffix(entry->d_name, PY_EXT_PY)) {
                size_t len = strlen(entry->d_name) - strlen(PY_EXT_PY);
                char modname[256];
                strncpy(modname, entry->d_name, len);
                modname[len] = '\0';
                add_module_name(modname);
            }
#ifdef PY_EXT_PREFIX
    #ifdef _WIN32
            else if (has_suffix(entry->d_name, PY_EXT_PYD) && match_ext_prefix(entry->d_name)) {
                size_t len = strlen(entry->d_name) - strlen(PY_EXT_PYD);
                char modname[256];
                strncpy(modname, entry->d_name, len);
                modname[len] = '\0';
                add_module_name(modname);
            }
    #else
            else if (has_suffix(entry->d_name, PY_EXT_SO) && match_ext_prefix(entry->d_name)) {
                const char* dot = strchr(entry->d_name, '.');
                if (dot) {
                    size_t len = dot - entry->d_name;
                    char modname[256];
                    strncpy(modname, entry->d_name, len);
                    modname[len] = '\0';
                    add_module_name(modname);
                }
            }
    #endif
#endif
        } else if (S_ISDIR(st.st_mode)) {
            char initfile[1024];
            snprintf(initfile, sizeof(initfile), "%s/__init__.py", fullpath);
            if (stat(initfile, &st) == 0) {
                add_module_name(entry->d_name);
            }
        }
    }
    closedir(dir);
#endif
}

int main() {


#ifdef PYTHON_STD_PATH
    scan_dir(PYTHON_STD_PATH);
#endif

#ifdef PYTHON_SITE_PATH
    scan_dir(PYTHON_SITE_PATH);
#endif
    for (int i = 0; builtin_modules[i] != NULL; i++) {
        printf("%s\n", builtin_modules[i]);
    }
    return 0;
}

```

